    <!-- PLUGIN MANIFEST -->
    <h3 id="plugin-manifest">Plugin Manifest &amp; Registration</h3>

    <Callout type="tip" title="What is a plugin?">
      A <strong>plugin</strong> is an add-on that gives Claude Code new abilities. Think of it like installing an app on your phone &mdash; your phone works fine without it, but the app adds features. VibeCrew is a plugin that adds project management, quality enforcement, and specialized AI agents to Claude Code. When you open a project with VibeCrew installed, Claude Code reads a small configuration file and automatically loads all the extra features.
    </Callout>

    <p>VibeCrew is a Claude Code plugin. Claude Code auto-detects it from the <code>.claude-plugin/plugin.json</code> manifest in the project root. The manifest registers all system components:</p>

    <CodeBlock code={pluginJson} lang="json" label="plugin.json" />

    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon purple">&#9632;</div>
        <h4>agents/</h4>
        <p>13 specialized agent prompt files. Each defines a model, isolation type, tool allowlist, and turn budget. Claude Code spawns them on demand.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon green">&#9654;</div>
        <h4>skills/</h4>
        <p>20 slash command definitions. Each is a <code>SKILL.md</code> file with YAML frontmatter that registers the command name, description, and arguments.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon amber">&#9881;</div>
        <h4>hooks/</h4>
        <p>Event-to-script routing table. 10 hook matchers across 6 lifecycle events, triggering bash scripts for zero-token enforcement.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon blue">&#9673;</div>
        <h4>.mcp.json + mcp-registry.json</h4>
        <p>MCP server configuration. 10 bundled servers in <code>.mcp.json</code> (3 enabled by default, 7 conditionally enabled from TDR). A registry of 25 servers in <code>templates/mcp-registry.json</code> enables discovery and injection of additional servers (Firebase, Clerk, MongoDB, etc.) based on TDR technology choices.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h4>Plugin file structure</h4>
    <pre><code><span class="code-label">layout</span>claude-plugin-vibe-crew/
  .claude-plugin/plugin.json    # Plugin manifest (entry point)
  .mcp.json                     # MCP server configuration
  settings.json                 # Permission rules (66 allowed, 22 denied)
  hooks/hooks.json              # Event-to-script routing table
  scripts/                      # ~67 bash automation scripts
  agents/                       # 14 specialized agent prompts
  skills/                       # 26 slash command definitions
  templates/                    # Badge catalog, challenge pool, quizzes, docs-site</code></pre>

    <!-- AGENTS -->
    <h3 id="agents">The 14-Agent Architecture</h3>

    <Callout type="tip" title="What are agents?">
      <p>Think of agents as <strong>specialist team members</strong>, each with a specific job. Instead of one AI doing everything, VibeCrew splits work across 14 agents &mdash; just like a real development team has designers, builders, testers, and reviewers. Each agent is an AI "personality" optimized for one task. The Builder writes code. The Verifier runs tests. The Security Auditor checks for vulnerabilities. You never interact with agents directly &mdash; VibeCrew picks the right one for each job automatically.</p>
      <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"><strong>Inline vs. Worktree:</strong> Some agents work directly in your project folder ("inline") &mdash; like a colleague sitting at your desk. Others work in a <strong>worktree</strong>, which is an isolated copy of your project files. Think of it as giving that specialist their own private office with a photocopy of all your documents. They can read, analyze, and write without affecting your real files. When they're done, only the finished result comes back to you. This isolation also saves your AI "memory" (context window) &mdash; all of their research stays in their office, not yours.</p>
    </Callout>

    <p>Each agent has a specific role, model selection, tool access, and context budget. Opus agents handle planning, research, code, security, and analysis &mdash; tasks where mistakes are expensive to fix later. Haiku agents handle fast, mechanical tasks (routing, running shell commands). Sonnet handles template-driven output like documentation.</p>

