    <Callout type="info" title="Script-only skills vs. agent-driven skills">
      Some skills (like <code>/idea</code>, <code>/status</code>, <code>/cost</code>) are pure script execution &mdash; they read/write JSON files and produce output with zero AI token cost. Others (like <code>/new-project</code>, <code>/wrap</code>) orchestrate multiple agents across the full workflow. The distinction keeps costs down: cheap operations stay cheap.
    </Callout>

    <!-- HOOKS -->
    <h3 id="hooks">The Hook System</h3>

    <Callout type="tip" title="What are hooks?">
      Hooks are <strong>automatic safety checks</strong> that run behind the scenes. Imagine a building with security guards at every door &mdash; when someone (an AI agent) tries to walk through (write a file, run a command), the guard (hook) checks if they're allowed. If yes, they pass through. If no, they're blocked with a clear message explaining why. Hooks are small programs that run instantly and cost nothing &mdash; they don't use any AI processing power. They're the reason VibeCrew can enforce rules like "no source code before the foundation is complete" without you having to remember or check yourself.
    </Callout>

    <p>Hooks are the enforcement layer. They run as bash scripts triggered by Claude Code lifecycle events. Because they're shell scripts, they consume <strong>zero AI context tokens</strong> &mdash; the most efficient enforcement mechanism possible.</p>

    <TableWrap>
