    <!-- SETTINGS -->
    <h3 id="settings">Settings &amp; Permissions</h3>

    <Callout type="tip" title="What are permissions?">
      Permissions control <strong>what the AI is and isn't allowed to do</strong> on your computer. VibeCrew pre-configures a list of safe operations (reading files, running tests, creating branches) and explicitly blocks dangerous ones (deleting everything, overwriting important data, installing system-wide software). This means the AI can work productively without asking you for permission every 5 seconds, while being physically unable to do anything destructive. It's like giving a contractor a key to the workshop but not the safe.
    </Callout>

    <p>The <code>settings.json</code> file defines tool-level permission rules enforced by Claude Code <em>before</em> any hook runs. This is the outermost security boundary.</p>

    <h4>Allowed tools (66 rules)</h4>
    <p>Standard development operations are pre-approved so the agent can work without constant permission prompts:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>File operations</strong> &mdash; All Read, Write, Edit, Glob, Grep</li>
      <li><strong>npm scripts</strong> &mdash; test, lint, build, format, type-check, audit</li>
      <li><strong>Git operations</strong> &mdash; status, log, diff, add, commit, checkout, branch, worktree, revert, reset --soft, tags</li>
      <li><strong>GitHub CLI</strong> &mdash; pr create/list/view, run list/view/watch; <strong>GitLab CLI</strong> &mdash; mr create/list/view, pipeline list/view</li>
      <li><strong>Shell utilities</strong> &mdash; jq, cat, ls, wc, sort, head, tail, mkdir, cp, mv</li>
      <li><strong>Security audits</strong> &mdash; npm audit, pip audit, bundle audit, govulncheck, cargo audit, composer audit</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Denied tools (22 rules)</h4>
    <p>Destructive operations are blocked at the permission layer &mdash; even if an agent tries to run them:</p>

    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon red">&#9888;</div>
        <h4>Elevation</h4>
        <p><code>sudo</code>, <code>su</code>, <code>doas</code>, <code>pkexec</code></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon red">&#9888;</div>
        <h4>Force operations</h4>
        <p><code>git push --force</code>, <code>git push -f</code>, <code>git reset --hard</code>, <code>git clean -f</code></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon red">&#9888;</div>
        <h4>Destructive</h4>
        <p><code>rm -rf /</code>, <code>rm -rf ~</code>, <code>rm -rf $HOME</code>, <code>chmod 777</code></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon red">&#9888;</div>
        <h4>System modifications</h4>
        <p><code>npm install -g</code>, <code>kill -9</code>, <code>systemctl</code>, <code>crontab</code>, <code>mkfs</code>, <code>dd</code></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Callout type="warn" title="Two layers of protection">
      The <code>settings.json</code> permission rules are the first layer (enforced by Claude Code). The <code>protect-data.sh</code> PreToolUse hook is the second layer (catches patterns like <code>DROP TABLE</code> that aren't tool-level commands). Together they form a defense-in-depth system.
    </Callout>

    <!-- ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAMS -->
    <h3 id="architecture-diagrams">Mermaid Architecture Diagram System</h3>

    <p>The 6th foundation artifact is a set of 5 Mermaid diagrams stored in <code>.vibecrew/architecture/</code>. They give agents a compact visual architecture reference at a fraction of the token cost of reading full-text documents.</p>

    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Mermaid Syntax</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>system.mmd</code></td><td><code>flowchart TD</code></td><td>Infrastructure topology with service boundaries</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>schema.mmd</code></td><td><code>erDiagram</code></td><td>Database entity-relationship diagram</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>state-flows.mmd</code></td><td><code>stateDiagram-v2</code></td><td>Authentication states and primary user journeys</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>api-sequences.mmd</code></td><td><code>sequenceDiagram</code></td><td>API request/response patterns</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>component-tree.mmd</code></td><td><code>flowchart TD</code></td><td>Component hierarchy with data flow direction</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p><strong>Lifecycle:</strong> Generated during Tier 1 (Step 5) from VISION.md + TDR + roadmap. At the start of each Tier 2 feature, the Workflow Orchestrator runs <code>inject-architecture.sh</code> to pre-load all 5 diagrams into context as a compressed architecture map. This gives every downstream agent instant architectural awareness without each loading diagrams independently. Updated during Tier 2 by the Builder (<code>component-tree.mmd</code>) and Doc Generator (all diagrams, during <code>/wrap</code>). The Code Reviewer checks diagram consistency as a warning-level finding.</p>

    <p><strong>Token savings:</strong> All 5 diagrams total ~250&ndash;600 tokens, compared to the TDR alone (2,000&ndash;5,000 tokens). Pre-loading them once via <code>inject-architecture.sh</code> eliminates redundant file reads across agents and enables faster, more accurate responses to architecture questions.</p>

    <!-- MCP SERVERS -->
    <h3 id="mcp-servers">MCP Server Integration</h3>

    <Callout type="tip" title="What are MCP servers?">
      MCP servers are <strong>external tools that give the AI extra abilities</strong>. Normally, the AI can only read your files and run commands. MCP servers connect it to additional services &mdash; like a reference library (Context7, for looking up documentation) or a web browser (Chrome DevTools, for visiting websites and debugging). They're optional: VibeCrew works fine without them, but with them, the AI works faster and uses less of its "memory" because it can look things up on demand instead of trying to remember everything.
    </Callout>

    <p>Nine MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers extend the agent's capabilities. Three ship enabled by default; six are conditionally enabled when the TDR selects matching technologies.</p>

    <h4>Always Enabled (no auth required)</h4>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon blue">&#9673;</div>
        <h4>Context7</h4>
        <p><strong>Documentation lookup.</strong> Instead of pasting library docs into chat (~1,500 tokens per paste), agents query Context7 for on-demand API references. Used by the Builder, Stack Scout, and Verifier.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon green">&#9673;</div>
        <h4>Chrome DevTools</h4>
        <p><strong>Browser debugging and automation.</strong> The Stack Scout uses it for web research and screenshot verification during TDR creation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon green">&#9673;</div>
        <h4>Playwright</h4>
        <p><strong>Interactive E2E testing.</strong> The Verifier uses it for browser debugging, selector exploration, and visual verification during E2E test development. Use <code>npx playwright test</code> for running full suites.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h4>Conditionally Enabled (auto-enabled from TDR)</h4>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon purple">&#9673;</div>
        <h4>Semgrep</h4>
        <p><strong>Static security analysis.</strong> Augments the Security Auditor's manual OWASP scanning with pattern-based vulnerability detection across large codebases. No auth required.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon purple">&#9673;</div>
        <h4>Sentry</h4>
        <p><strong>Production error context.</strong> The CI Healer queries Sentry for stack traces and error frequency to enrich CI failure diagnosis. Requires <code>SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon amber">&#9673;</div>
        <h4>Supabase</h4>
        <p><strong>Database schema inspection.</strong> Stack Scout and Builder use it for schema inspection, migrations, and seed data. <strong>Never use against production.</strong> Requires <code>SUPABASE_URL</code> and <code>SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon amber">&#9673;</div>
        <h4>Stripe</h4>
        <p><strong>Payment product management.</strong> Builder uses it for creating products, prices, and verifying webhook configurations. <strong>Always use test mode keys.</strong> Requires <code>STRIPE_SECRET_KEY</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon amber">&#9673;</div>
        <h4>Vercel</h4>
        <p><strong>Deployment management.</strong> Builder uses it for project configuration, deployment status, and environment variable inspection. Requires <code>VERCEL_TOKEN</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon amber">&#9673;</div>
        <h4>Figma</h4>
        <p><strong>Design spec extraction.</strong> Builder uses it to extract design specifications, spacing values, and component structures from Figma files. Requires <code>FIGMA_ACCESS_TOKEN</code>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>Servers are toggled via <code>scripts/enable-mcp-server.sh &lt;name&gt; [enable|disable]</code>. After TDR approval, <code>scripts/sync-mcp-from-tdr.sh</code> auto-enables bundled servers and recommends additional servers from the registry. Run <code>scripts/recommend-mcp-servers.sh</code> to see recommendations, or <code>scripts/add-mcp-server.sh &lt;key&gt;</code> to add a server from the registry (Firebase, Prisma, Clerk, Auth0, Netlify, Railway, Shopify, MongoDB, Resend, Next.js, shadcn/ui, Terraform, Kubernetes, Neon, Upstash Redis).</p>

    <Callout type="tip" title="Token savings from Context7">
      A single session might query Supabase auth docs, Next.js routing docs, and Tailwind utility references. Without Context7, that's ~4,500 tokens of pasted documentation. With Context7, the agent fetches exactly what it needs on demand &mdash; and those tokens are served from the MCP server, not your context window. The Performance Coach tracks this and deducts points (-15) for low cache utilization when Context7 isn't being used.
    </Callout>

    <Callout type="info" title="MCP Tool Search (automatic)">
      Claude Code's built-in Tool Search (January 2026) dynamically loads only 3&ndash;5 relevant tool definitions per task instead of preloading all MCP tools into context. It activates automatically when combined tool definitions exceed 10% of the context window. For VibeCrew projects that enable 5+ servers after TDR selection, this prevents tool definitions from consuming context &mdash; reducing overhead from ~60K tokens to ~8.5K. VibeCrew's &ldquo;disabled by default, enable from TDR&rdquo; pattern complements this: baseline projects start with only 3 servers, and Tool Search handles the scaling.
    </Callout>
